menu "Pocket VM (L0 measurement probes)"

    config POCKET_VM_SELFTEST
        bool "Enable USB L lifecycle self-test"
        depends on POCKET_VM_YIELD
        default n
        help
            Runs the host lifecycle test on the device via USB command L.
            Use a private sdkconfig in a dedicated build directory.

    config KSN_DEVICE_PROBE
        bool "Enable USB-triggered Kasane core regression and LCD diagnostic"
        default n
        help
            USB '~' on the home screen runs tests and opaque rectangles. Uses extra
            static storage and stack. Not a production renderer benchmark.

    config POCKET_VM_PROBE
        bool "Enable L0 VM probes (docs/vm/quickjs-freertos-vm-spec.md sec.5)"
        default n
        help
            Off by default and meant to stay that way for every normal build.
            On, this compiles in:
              - main/pocket/vmprobe.c, which samples the guest's JS memory
                usage, free heap, largest free block and ui_task's C stack
                high-water mark every 8th frame (frame time and job counts
                every frame) and logs a rate-limited
                "VMPROBE ..." summary (min/median/max over a ~1s window)
                instead of one line per frame.
              - a #ifdef-guarded probe block inside the vendored
                components/quickjs-ng/quickjs-ng/quickjs.c that exposes
                sizeof(JSStackFrame)/sizeof(JSVarRef) (private to that file)
                and a job-queue depth counter around JS_EnqueueJob /
                JS_ExecutePendingJob.
            The six USB-only L0 workloads that used to live under apps/vmprobe/
            (letters A-F) were retired once L0 finished (tag vm-L0);
            `git checkout vm-L0 -- apps/vmprobe tools/vm_l0_capture.py`
            brings them back for a re-measurement. With this on, the probe
            above now samples whatever app is actually running.
            Build a separate build_* directory with this on -- and give that
            directory its OWN sdkconfig file (-D SDKCONFIG=<dir>/sdkconfig;
            see sdkconfig.vmprobe.defaults), because ESP-IDF's default
            sdkconfig path is the project root, shared by every build_*
            directory regardless of -B. Never turn this on in
            sdkconfig.defaults or in the shared root sdkconfig.

    config POCKET_VM_SCHED
        bool "Job-boundary scheduling and wake (docs/vm/vm-L1-design.md, L1)"
        default y
        help
            The L1 execution control. On, a turn's Promise job drain may stop
            BETWEEN two jobs once the turn's time budget is spent, and the rest
            of that drain runs at the top of the next turn before any host
            event or frame() reaches JavaScript; a completion posted from
            another task can also cut short the frame-cap sleep instead of
            waiting out the whole period.

            Off, every knob it adds is set to "unlimited": the drain cannot
            yield, so no continuation turn can exist, the unhandled-rejection
            report point is the one it always was, and the frame cap is the old
            unconditional vTaskDelay. That is the pre-L1 behaviour exactly,
            which is what spec sec.12 means by every stage being revertible --
            if a board run finds something this level broke, this switch puts
            it back without reverting the code.

            Measure the cost with tools/memlog.py rather than assuming it.

    config POCKET_VM_SEGFRAMES
        bool "JS frames in runtime-owned segments, not on the C stack (L2a)"
        default y
        help
            docs/vm/quickjs-freertos-vm-spec.md sec.7 L2a, docs/vm/vm-L2-design.md.
            On, JS_CallInternal takes its JSStackFrame and the block that
            used to be alloca()'d (arguments, locals, operand stack, var_refs)
            from a bump-allocated segment chain the runtime owns
            (components/quickjs-ng/quickjs-ng/quickjs-vmstack.h). Segments come
            from the guest heap through js_malloc_rt, so they count against
            JS_SetMemoryLimit; one 4 KiB segment stays resident from the first
            call to JS_FreeRuntime, a frame that does not fit chains another,
            and a frame larger than a segment gets one of its own.

            What this changes for an app: per JS call the ui task's 20 KiB C
            stack loses the frame (48 B) and the locals block, keeping only
            JS_CallInternal's own C frame -- and with POCKET_VM_FLATCALLS
            (L2b, below) not even that for a JS-to-JS call. Deep recursion
            still ends in the same RangeError from JS_SetMaxStackSize; a
            frame the heap refuses is an InternalError "out of memory", the
            error a generator's heap frame already produced. Generator /
            async frames are untouched.

            JS_SetMaxStackSize (app_session.c: 20 KiB) is read twice on this
            path (design D10): as the C-stack limit it always was, and as a
            byte budget for the segments -- the sum of every live frame, in
            bytes, not a frame count. Whichever fills first raises the
            RangeError; the budget is tested before the heap is asked, so
            "too deep" stays RangeError and only a frame the heap cannot
            hold is InternalError. The budget is the guard that survives
            L2b's removal of the C recursion. tools/vmtest/budget_probe.sh
            shows it working with the C-stack guard lifted.

            Off, JS_CallInternal is the upstream alloca path and JSRuntime
            has no segment state: the pre-L2a behaviour exactly, which is
            what spec sec.12 means by every stage being revertible. The host
            harness builds the same switch as tools/vmtest/build.sh
            "-alloca" variants.

    config POCKET_VM_FLATCALLS
        bool "JS-to-JS calls stay in one JS_CallInternal activation (L2b)"
        depends on POCKET_VM_SEGFRAMES
        default y
        help
            docs/vm/quickjs-freertos-vm-spec.md sec.7 L2b, docs/vm/vm-L2-design.md
            sec.10. On, a call opcode whose target is a bytecode function
            (call / call0-3 / call_method / tail_call / tail_call_method)
            pushes the callee's frame on the segment stack and keeps running
            in the SAME C activation of JS_CallInternal; the callee's return
            pops that frame and resumes the caller from the frame chain. No
            C stack is consumed per JS level, so the recursion limit is the
            segment byte budget (D10) alone -- the JS_SetMaxStackSize value,
            in bytes of live frames -- and a frame the heap cannot hold is
            the InternalError it already was. Constructor calls, apply,
            eval, and every C-to-JS re-entry (a builtin calling back into
            JS, getters, Proxy traps) still recurse in C and still meet the
            C-stack guard at the entry of the new activation: those are
            "wrapped" synchronous calls in the spec's sense, marked
            interruption-forbidden.

            Costs, per frame pushed on a segment: one pointer (the caller's
            operand stack pointer, D12) in front of the JSStackFrame, and
            three fields folded into JSStackFrame's padding (48 B on the
            target either way). Per C activation: three saved pointers.

            Off, every JS-to-JS call is a C recursion of JS_CallInternal as
            in L2a (the C-stack guard answers first, exactly as before).
            The host harness builds this switch as the "-flat" / "-recur"
            variants of tools/vmtest/build.sh. Default y since every host
            gate passed on it (design sec.10.5); NOT yet run on the board,
            like L2a itself (design sec.8.4) -- that is what the switch is
            for.

    config POCKET_VM_YIELD
        bool "Suspend and resume JS at audited opcode safepoints (L2c)"
        depends on POCKET_VM_FLATCALLS
        default y
        help
            Implements the L2c save-and-resume path described by
            docs/vm/vm-L2-design.md sec.11. On since 2026-09-23: the
            mechanism landed stage by stage, and what it costs has been
            measured rather than guessed (docs/vm/vm-L2-results.md sec.8) --
            +0.05 ms on the JS turn (0.17 -> 0.22 on hello, three reflashes
            each), +3,784 B of flash, +32 B of static DRAM. The frame guard
            it brings charges a frame's accumulated execution time against
            VM_FRAME_RUNAWAY_US; the five shipped JS apps spend at most
            47.4 ms in one frame, a factor of 5.3 under it, and a frame at
            239.6 ms completes while one at 256.0 ms is stopped.

            With it off, forced VM stops retain the pre-L2c uncatchable
            interrupt and there is no frame guard at all. Turning it off is
            still the way to compare against that path; it is not a smaller
            build in any sense that matters.

    config POCKET_VM_RELOC
        bool "Relocatable frame segments: the explicit move API (L3a)"
        depends on POCKET_VM_YIELD
        default n
        help
            Compiles in JS_VMStackRelocate, which copies the live frame
            segments to fresh addresses and rewrites every pointer that
            named the old ones (docs/vm/vm-L3-design.md, the entry ledger
            is docs/vm/vm-ledger/09-relocation-entries.md).

            Moving is only legal while the VM is parked at an L2c
            safepoint: a running JS_CallInternal keeps pointers into the
            block in C locals and registers, and the spec rules out
            guessing which machine words those are. That is why this
            depends on POCKET_VM_YIELD rather than on SEGFRAMES alone.

            The only caller is the diagnostic one: USB '&' on the home
            screen arms the next app, guest_continue_impl() then moves the
            segments before every resume, and the session logs VM_RELOC
            with what that cost. tools/vmtest/device_reloc.py drives it.
            Unarmed, a RELOC build takes the same path as a build without
            it.

            That is deliberately not a reason to move: deciding WHEN to
            relocate belongs to L4, which has to show first that the
            non-moving segments are not already enough. Off by default and
            meant to stay that way until that case is made.

            Costs, measured on this tree: flash +1,300 B, static DRAM
            +16 B against the same build with it off.

    config POCKET_VM_ROM_ATOMS
        bool "F1: builtin names in flash instead of heap strings"
        default y
        help
            Every JS runtime used to start by copying ~540 builtin names
            (property and method names of the intrinsics) from flash into
            heap JSStrings: ~21.7 KB of every app's guest heap, identical in
            every run (docs/vm/builtin-floor-plan.md). With this on they
            stay in flash as records generated at build time
            (components/quickjs-ng/quickjs-ng/quickjs-rom-atoms.h, from
            tools/vmtest/floor/gen_rom_atoms.sh); a name becomes a heap
            string only when a program asks for it as a string value.

            Costs ~15.5 KB of flash. Regenerate the table after changing the
            builtins; a stale table is still correct, it only saves less.

            Default y since 2026-09-25: every shipped app's js= fell by
            20.1-21.4 KB on the device, corpus/Test262 unchanged
            (docs/vm/builtin-floor-plan.md sec.12). n builds the old path.

    config POCKET_VM_OOMPROBE
        bool "G12 fragmentation probe: log every failed heap allocation"
        default n
        help
            The device half of the L3/L4 decision (docs/vm/backlog.md G12):
            does fragmentation make allocations fail here? Registers the
            heap's failed-allocation hook, which walks the heap at the moment
            of every refusal and records, per heap region, whether the free
            bytes would have held the request had they been contiguous
            ("G12 FAIL ... cand=1"). Also logs every JS_TakeOOMCanary report
            ("G12 CANARY") and a per-session total ("G12 SESSION").

            Each refusal also says whether treating the VM's frame segments
            as movable would have left a long enough run ("segfix=1"):
            L3/L4 move segments and nothing else, so that is the case they
            could prevent. Adds JS_VMStackBlocks' only caller.

            Adds USB workloads on the home screen that drive the guest into
            OOM on purpose: '!' interleaved holes plus a growing large
            request, '@' a growing array between small objects, '#' JSON
            round-trips of a growing array, and '$' the negative control --
            a heap where the segments are what splits the free space.
            tools/vmtest/device_g12.py runs them and the shipped apps.

            Measurement only. Build a separate build_* directory with its own
            sdkconfig (-D SDKCONFIG=<dir>/sdkconfig).

    config POCKET_VM_TCO
        bool "Experimental strict tail-call frame reuse"
        depends on POCKET_VM_YIELD
        default n
        help
            Reuse flat segment frames at strict tail calls to ordinary
            bytecode functions. Sloppy calls and unsupported shapes keep
            the existing path. Changes strict Error.stack and recursion
            limits, so this stays opt-in during compatibility validation.

    config POCKET_VM_FAIR
        bool "Fair ordering: run the pumps on a continuation turn too"
        default n
        help
            OFF (the shipping default) is COMPAT ORDERING, the rule
            docs/vm/vm-L1-design.md sec.2.1 states: a turn that ended with jobs
            queued finishes them at the top of the next turn, and no host event
            reaches JavaScript until the queue is empty. One logical drain then
            has exactly the job order the pre-L1 single drain produced, and the
            only thing that changed is that wall clock passes between two of
            its jobs. This switch compiles to nothing when it is off.

            ON, a continuation turn that STILL has jobs left runs the same
            pumps an ordinary turn runs -- everything except frame() and the
            exit() check -- after its drain has yielded. A completion that a
            radio, a bus or a timer recorded during the drain is settled there
            and its reaction is enqueued at the TAIL of the queue, behind the
            jobs of the unfinished drain, so FIFO inside the queue is exactly
            what it was; what changes is only that a host event can reach
            JavaScript between two jobs of one logical drain.

            The cost compat ordering pays is completion latency on an app whose
            drain needs continuations -- measured (device) at ~1.9x the pre-L1
            path under audio contention (docs/vm/vm-L1-report.md sec.2.8). This mode
            exists so that tradeoff can be measured rather than argued, and so
            L2 can revisit it with evidence. Two things are deliberately NOT
            made fair, because neither can be made safe at a job boundary:
            exit() (delivered as an uncatchable interrupt, it would cut the
            drain it lands in) and frame() (it would put a frame inside a
            chain). See docs/vm/vm-L1-report.md sec.9.3.

    config POCKET_UI_TASK_CORE
        int "Core the ui task is pinned to (-1 = let FreeRTOS choose)"
        range -1 1
        default 1
        help
            main/main.c's ui_task owns the whole UI, every screen and the JS
            session (docs/vm/vm-L1-design.md sec.4.1), so where it runs decides
            where every JS job runs. -1 creates it with tskNO_AFFINITY, which
            is what xTaskCreate() did here before; 0 or 1 pins it.

            The default is 1 because that is what the board measured
            (docs/vm/vm-L1-report.md sec.8, all 実測(device)): core 1 is
            indistinguishable from no affinity -- the JS turn medians match to
            0.1%, the LCD transfer to 0.04%, DIRAM to the byte -- while core 0
            costs 5.6% of a compute-bound turn, 7.6% of the transfer, and 16%
            of an HTTP request running on the other core, because core 0 is
            where ESP-IDF fixes the Wi-Fi driver task. Pinning also removes the
            one migration the board does take (measured: 1 in 1,800 idle
            frames, docs/vm/vm-L1-report.md sec.8.8), which is what makes a per-core
            clock (CONFIG_POCKET_VM_CCOUNT) possible at all.

            The three values are the SAME binary with one immediate changed --
            xTaskCreatePinnedToCore() is the only call site either way -- so a
            measurement across them is not confounded by a code-path change.

    config POCKET_VM_CCOUNT
        bool "Read CCOUNT, not esp_timer, for the L1 job budget"
        default n
        help
            The L1 turn budget asks the clock how much of its 8 ms is left,
            once every VM_JOB_STRIDE jobs (vm_sched.h). CCOUNT --
            the per-core cycle counter, RSR CCOUNT, the one main/scene/flower.c
            already uses -- costs 25 ns a read against esp_timer_get_time()'s
            833 ns (measured (device), docs/vm/vm-L1-report.md sec.8.7).

            It is per core, so it is only sound when the task that runs the
            drain cannot migrate: components/pocketjs_guest/src/vm_clock.c
            refuses to compile unless CONFIG_POCKET_UI_TASK_CORE >= 0.

            Whether it is worth having is a measurement, not a preference --
            docs/vm/vm-L1-report.md sec.8 records what each clock costs on the
            job-heavy workloads.

    config POCKET_VM_LAZY_INPUTS
        bool "Lazy restoration of flat call inputs"
        depends on POCKET_VM_FLATCALLS
        default y
        help
            Reconstruct argc/argv/this/new.target on their next bytecode use,
            instead of at every flat return. Disable to restore eagerly.

    config POCKET_VM_CALLBENCH
        bool "Diagnostic same-binary flat/recursive call benchmark"
        depends on POCKET_VM_FLATCALLS && !POCKET_VM_YIELD
        select POCKET_VM_LAZY_INPUTS
        default n
        help
            Adds an idle-runtime dispatch selector and the USB N benchmark.
            Use an isolated build directory and SDKCONFIG. This compares the
            two dispatch paths inside the flat interpreter, not two complete
            compile-time engine configurations. Never enable for shipping.

    config POCKET_VM_L1_CLOCKBENCH
        bool "Enable L1 clock-cost bench (docs/vm/vm-L1-report.md 8.7)"
        default n
        help
            A throwaway bench for one question: what does reading the clock
            cost, and does the ui task migrate cores? NOT part of any normal
            build. On, this compiles in:
              - a boot-time loop (main.c) that times esp_cpu_get_cycle_count()
                (RSR CCOUNT) against esp_timer_get_time() (systimer), logging
                "CLOCKBENCH ..." lines once, then falls through to the normal
                boot.
              - a per-frame xPortGetCoreID() migration counter in ui_task,
                logging a rate-limited "BENCH_CORE ..." summary.
            Same isolation rule as POCKET_VM_PROBE: give the build directory
            its own -D SDKCONFIG=<dir>/sdkconfig, never enable this in the
            shared root sdkconfig or in sdkconfig.defaults. See
            sdkconfig.vmclockbench.defaults.

    config POCKET_VM_STRIP_FN_SOURCE
        bool "Do not keep function source text in the guest heap"
        default y
        help
            Upstream QuickJS copies the full source text of every function it
            compiles (an inner function's text a second time, inside its
            parent's) so that Function.prototype.toString can return it. On
            the 160 KiB guest those copies measured 2.5-7.1 KiB per app
            (docs/kasane/decisions.md, docs/vm/vm-L2-results.md
            sec.6), and nothing else reads them.

            On (default), no copy is made. What is lost: toString of a JS
            function returns upstream's no-source fallback,
            "function <name>() {\n    [native code]\n}", for every kind of
            function (async, generator, arrow and class included), instead of
            its source. Code that parses a function's own text breaks; calling,
            error messages, stack traces and line numbers are unaffected.

            Off, the upstream behaviour exactly. The host harness mirrors the
            default; tools/vmtest/build.sh's "-keepsrc" suffix builds it off.

endmenu
